return [
    'credentials_heading' => 'Credentials',
    'credentials_p1' => 'ESXi and Ansible credentials are stored encrypted and are offered as a choice when queuing a deploy job. An Ansible credential is only the SSH account for the execution host. The API base URL under Settings is the separate callback address to the portal, not an alternative credential; a deploy uses the selected SSH credential and the effective URL together. Without at least one ESXi and one Ansible credential, plus a reachable API base URL, no job can be queued.',
    'deploy_heading' => 'Deploy modes',
    'deploy_requirements_p1' => 'A mission can only be queued once it has at least one VM and a datastore set; only the "Apply ESXi autostart policy" mode works without a datastore. Only one job runs per mission at a time; another is possible only after it finishes or its cancellation is confirmed.',
    'deploy_requirements_p2' => 'A job also needs a mission, an ESXi credential, an Ansible credential and an API base URL. If any of them is missing, the form names exactly the missing ones one by one and links to the page where each is created; the "Queue job" button stays greyed out until then. Anyone who may not open the linked page still sees the reason, just without the link.',
    'deploy_p1' => 'Queuing a deploy job requires choosing one of these modes:',
    'deploy_mode_full' => 'Full pipeline: complete run. Create VMs, power-cycle, export MACs, start. When the mission enabled autostart, the autostart policy is written last.',
    'deploy_mode_autostart' => 'Apply ESXi autostart policy: writes the mission autostart policy to the host, without creating or starting VMs.',
    'deploy_mode_create' => 'Create VMs: creates the VMs on ESXi without starting them. An existing VM with the same name is only reused when its instance UUID already belongs to this portal VM; a foreign or not-yet-known namesake blocks the run instead of having its hardware aligned.',
    'deploy_mode_powercycle' => 'Power-Cycle + Export MACs: briefly powers VMs on and off again so ESXi assigns MAC addresses, then reads them out. It only touches a VM whose PXE network interface has no MAC in the portal yet and that was powered off when the run started; running or suspended VMs keep their state, and only what this run itself started is powered off again.',
    'deploy_mode_export' => 'Export MACs: reads MAC addresses from VMs that are already running.',
    'deploy_mode_start' => 'Start VMs: starts VMs that already exist.',
    'deploy_verbose_heading' => 'Ansible -vvv',
    'deploy_verbose_p1' => 'The "Ansible -vvv" option raises Ansible output to triple verbosity (-vvv). The deploy log then additionally shows every single module action, the variables used and SSH connection details. This helps with troubleshooting but produces significantly more log lines and should stay off during normal operation.',
    'deploy_powercycle_wait_heading' => 'Power-cycle wait',
    'deploy_powercycle_wait_p1' => 'The wait time sits between powering on and the hard power-off of the power-cycle: the VMs are switched on, ESXi assigns the MAC addresses during the wait, then they are switched off again. It applies only to the "Full pipeline" and "Power-Cycle + Export MACs" modes; in every other mode the form disables the field because no power-cycle happens.',
    'deploy_start_wait_heading' => 'Wait before start',
    'deploy_start_wait_p1' => 'This pause sits in front of powering the VMs on. It gives MECM the time to take the freshly registered devices into their collections, so the VM finds a task sequence when it PXE-boots. How long that takes depends on the MECM cadence of the environment, which is why this is a form field and not a fixed value. The default is :default seconds.',
    'deploy_start_wait_p2' => 'The wait applies only to the "Full pipeline" and "Start VMs" modes; in every other mode the form disables the field. Its upper bound is :max seconds, and that bound is a necessity rather than caution: the job waits across an SSH connection that aborts after :idle seconds without output. A longer pause would fail the job in exactly the step that powers the VMs on, and they would be left switched off.',
    'deploy_identity_heading' => 'VM identity and adoption',
    'deploy_identity_p1' => 'The name only selects a candidate on ESXi; the instance UUID proves which VM it really is. Before create, power-cycle, start and autostart, the Ansible run checks name and UUID again on the host. MAC export rejects a differing result completely before it can write MACs or status back to the portal. A changed MOID alone is harmless: it is only the host\'s current handle and may change when a VM is registered again.',
    'deploy_identity_p2' => 'When inventory finds a namesake without the matching stored UUID, “Queue job” stays blocked. First verify the VM in the ESXi Host Client. Only if it really belongs to this portal record, use the explicitly confirmed “Adopt identity” action; it stores MOID and instance UUID but changes neither hardware nor power state and starts no deploy job. Never adopt a foreign VM.',
    'deploy_schedule_heading' => 'Schedule and staggering',
    'deploy_schedule_p1' => 'A job starts immediately or at a set time. Times are in the portal timezone; internally scheduling is in UTC. A scheduled job is only claimed by the worker at its time; if the worker is offline it is caught up afterwards in order.',
    'deploy_schedule_p2' => 'Staggering creates one job per VM that start one after another every N minutes (full, power-cycle and start modes only). In the job list they carry a batch marker and their scheduled time.',
    'deploy_schedule_p3' => 'When a time or a stagger is set, a preview of the computed start times appears before queuing. "Cancel group" stops all still-waiting jobs of a batch; a job that is already running finishes.',
    'deploy_cancel_heading' => 'Cancelling a job',
    'deploy_cancel_p1' => 'A waiting job ends immediately when cancelled. For a running job the cancel is first only requested (status "cancelling"): the worker finishes the playbook step that is currently executing, because a hard stop in the middle of a VM creation would leave the ESXi host in a state no later step can reason about. At the next step boundary the worker confirms the cancellation; only then the job reads "cancelled".',
    'deploy_cancel_p2' => 'While the confirmation is pending the job stays active: the mission cannot be deleted, no new job can be queued, and a MAC report still arriving from that step is accepted; VMs already imported stay deployed. If the worker dies before confirming, the supervision detects the cancellation shortly after and concludes it itself.',
    'deploy_storage_heading' => 'Storage requirements when queuing',
    'deploy_storage_p1' => 'As soon as a mission is selected, the form shows how much disk space the job will use per target datastore. It counts the provisioned sizes of all disks of the checked VMs, so ten machines at the default size of :size each add up to ten times that. VMs with their own datastore get their own row. A VM without its own disks is created with the default disk (:size, :type) and counted at that size in the storage estimate.',
    'deploy_storage_p2' => 'Once an ESXi credential is chosen, the table compares the requirement against the free space of the last inventory pull and marks every row as sufficient or as probably too small. Unchecking VMs recalculates the totals immediately. If the credential reports no free space for a datastore, the assessment stays open; a missing number is never read as 0 anywhere.',
    'deploy_storage_p3' => 'This is a snapshot and never blocks a job. The numbers are as old as the last pull, and thin disks initially use less than their provisioned size. For a scheduled job the same table appears again in the schedule preview before you confirm.',
    'deploy_storage_p4' => 'A datastore in maintenance mode is marked as such, and its free space does not count: the assessment stays open instead of promising room where nothing can be created right now. The same holds for a datastore the credential reports as not reachable. If a pull does not deliver these facts at all, nothing is guessed; the numbers stay as reported, and a job then fails visibly on the ESXi host rather than going wrong silently.',
    'deploy_warn_heading' => 'Inventory warnings when queuing',
    'deploy_warn_p1' => 'Three separate hints, none blocks: the mission warning appears when a value of the mission is not in the inventory of any host (value probably stale). The host warning appears live while choosing the ESXi credential, when a value exists on other hosts but not on the selected one (check the host choice); the same value never appears in both hints. Also while choosing the host, the capability hint appears when the selected host reports restrictions, for example a free license (no deploy, no autostart) or an HA cluster (autostart without effect).',
    'deploy_warn_p2' => 'Mission deploys claim the queue before inventory fetches. Waiting fetches therefore never overtake a deploy; only a fetch that is already running still finishes, because the worker executes exactly one job at a time.',
    'deploy_warn_p3' => 'Ignoring a host warning does not block the job: it starts, but creating the VMs fails on a host that lacks the named datastore or datacenter. The job then ends as "failed" with the module message in the log. The warnings are deliberately only the early signal; blocking happens only in the two named cases (missing datacenter without an unambiguous inventory answer, freshly proven free licence), never because of a stale value.',
    'deploy_retry_heading' => 'Running a failed job again',
    'deploy_retry_p1' => 'A failed, cancelled or partially successful job (status "partial": some VMs finished, the rest did not) gets the "Run again" button in the job list. A succeeded job is queued through the start form instead, so that the readiness preview runs once more; a running job is cancelled, not retried. The automatic inventory pulls of the system cannot be retried, they schedule themselves.',
    'deploy_retry_p2' => 'Running it again creates a new job with the same content and the same credentials; the old job and its log stay, and the new log points back to it. The new job starts immediately, even when the original one was scheduled or part of a staggered group, and it is attributed to the person who ran it again. Exception "partial": there the new job only repeats the MAC export of the failed VMs, not the whole deployment.',
    'deploy_retry_p3' => 'Before it is queued, the same checks run as for a fresh job: a mission may only have one active job, and mission, VMs and credentials must still exist. If any of them was deleted since the first attempt, running it again fails with a message instead of producing a half-valid job.',
    'autostart_heading' => 'Autostart on the ESXi host',
    'autostart_p1' => 'The "Start VMs" mode powers the VMs on once. The "Apply ESXi autostart policy" mode instead writes them into the host autostart list for good, so they come up on their own after a reboot or a power loss. It is the same list the host client shows under "Manage, System, Autostart".',
    'autostart_inherit_heading' => 'Inheritance: the mission sets the default, a VM may differ',
    'autostart_inherit_p1' => 'The mission carries the host defaults, each VM optionally its own. Leave a delay field on the VM blank and the mission value applies (default :seconds seconds). Change the mission value later and, on the next autostart run, every VM that set nothing of its own follows.',
    'autostart_inherit_p2' => 'Careful: 0 means "no wait" and is not the same as "inherit". A blank field inherits, a 0 starts immediately.',
    'autostart_limits_heading' => 'Where autostart has no effect',
    'autostart_limit_license' => 'Free ESXi licence: the API is read-only. The job aborts with a clear message instead of appearing to succeed.',
    'autostart_limit_ha' => 'A host inside a vSphere HA cluster: ESXi disables autostart there. The HA restart priority controls the order instead.',
    'autostart_limit_tools' => 'The "shut down the guest" action and the heartbeat option need VMware Tools in the guest. Without them the stop delay elapses and the VM is powered off hard, and the heartbeat option buys no time.',
    'autostart_heartbeat_heading' => 'What the heartbeat option does',
    'autostart_heartbeat_p1' => 'The host starts the VMs one after another. Without the option it waits out the full start delay between two VMs, even when the previous one has long been up. With the option it powers on the next VM as soon as the VMware Tools of the previous one report in; the start delay then only acts as an upper bound in case nothing reports.',
    'autostart_heartbeat_p2' => 'Turn it on when VMware Tools run in every VM of the mission: the boot sequence gets shorter and the order more reliable, because the next VM only starts once the previous one is really there, not merely powered on. Turn it off when a VM without Tools is in the set, or when a service needs post-boot time the Tools do not capture: fixed delays are more honest then.',
    'autostart_limit_order' => 'There is no field for a start number: an open bug in the Ansible collection rejects any number above 1. The order follows from the start delays.',
    'autostart_limit_shared_host' => 'When VMs of several missions live on one host, they share its defaults and the last mission written wins them. That is why the delays can be overridden per VM.',
    'autostart_run_heading' => 'Applying and withdrawing',
    'autostart_run_p1' => 'The mode only writes the VMs selected in the job. A VM whose checkbox was cleared loses its entry on the next run that includes it, not before.',
    'autostart_run_p1b' => 'A VM only participates when its own checkbox is set AND the mission has autostart switched on. While the mission switch is off, the autostart fields in the VM editor are locked; a value already set stays stored and takes effect once the mission is switched on.',
    'autostart_run_p2' => 'The full pipeline appends the autostart step only for a mission that enabled it. The host autostart switch is never turned off, because VMs of other missions may live on it.',
